Date: Mon, 03 Jan 1994 23:08:26 -0700 (MST)
From: ASIST@ACVAX.INRE.ASU.EDU
Subject: FOUNDATIONS - the "name continuum"

I don't know where this thread comes from, but I have some thoughts
here and now on the "name continuum."
        I understand the "name continuum" like this:  First of all,
you just can't introduce a concept into the mainstream without its
losing its integrity.  Grunge at the Gap, the Monkees, whatever.
That's the way it is.  I think that that is why people write their
autobiographies or go to confession.  If the public knows your
nastiest secrets, accepts your nasty secrets as part of the culture,
your nasties becomes public property. That might also be why people on
the extropian list debate "outreach."  Extropianism would have to
appeal to some lower common denomonator in order to introduce itself
into the public.  Second of all, the only way to get the public's
attention is to present something new and different.  This is how art
works.  Take poetry for instance, which will always say the same old
things - poets don't invent new emotions, they simply defamiliarize
the old ones so that the reader can experience them.
Defamiliarization is simply introducing an old idea so that it is not
immediatly apparant that that is what it is.
        The "name continuum," it seems to me, is an attempt to
defamiliarize in order to maintain integrity.  The problem is that
once the new name becomes mainstream, it loses its integrity and must
be changed again.  The silly thing about the "name continuum" is that
it wants both things at once - to be mainstream and to be distinct
from the mainstream.   The defamiliarization works because whenever
someone says "African American" that person is aware that they are not
saying "Black."  It is that sort of constant consiousness that the
"name continuum" wants to achieve.  But it wants to make certain that
the consciouness becomes mainstream, which is impossible because there
is nothing conscious about mainstream.  The new name will lose its
definition (in both senses) eventually; consciousness will dissolve in
the mainstream, and a new name will take it's place and suffer the
same fate.  That's what you get if you look to the dominant culture
for your affirmation. - Ilse Thompson  

