Date:     Tue, 4 Jan 94 6:52:46 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  ECO: Evidence Supporting Free Markets

>From: "Harry S. Hawk" <habs@panix.com>
>Subject: ECO: Japan as proof of free markets
>
>Japan as proof of the free market working.
>
>A debate that raged on this list perhaps a year ago, centered on if we
>could ever have a scientific test of the free market. The debate
>raised the issue, "could you have something like a double-blind
>controlled study of markets." Since such a study seems unlikely a
>deeper question remained, "is their anything equivalent to it?"

The best cases studied thus far are those of Hong Kong and Singapore.
Both low on natural resources, high on population, similar ethnic
composition of population (mostly ethnic Chinese), both former Brit
colonies, both with free trade.  Difference: Singapore has industrial
policy, Hong Kong doesn't.

Singapore also compels saving into the State provident fund, which
gives it a 40% savings rate.  Hong Kong's savings rate is 20%.  So
Singapore has essentially twice the input of Hong Kong.

If they were equally effient at allocating resources, then Singapore
would also have twice the output.  It doesn't.  The output per capita
of the two countries is roughly the same.  This means that, on the average,
Singapore's industrial policy allocates resources about half as well as
does Hong Kong's free market.  Actually, Singapore's past the point of
diminishing returns, meaning that it takes more and more capital at the
margin to get more output.  Hong Kong shows no signs of reaching that
point at any time in the forseeable future.

And Singapore makes most of the rest of the world look like wimps when
it comes to economic growth!  It's the best State-managed economy in
the world, and the free market still beats it all to hell.

