1)Isolation: yes, if an individual has previously committed multiple crimes
with real victims, and if my estimate that he/she is more than 50% likely to
do so again, I do not find this an inadequate justification for some form
of physical restraint.

2) Rehabilitation: if an individual does not wish to be rehabilitated, fine --
so long as full restitution is made and some other form of protection against
repeat offences is available.  And I agree, prisons do a miserable job at this.

3) Restitution: incarceration is not required to ensure this in most cases,
but may be in some -- my previous comments re: chain gangs apply.

4) Retribution: consider the possibility that the emotion which desires this
has evolutionary value -- as a deterrent.  Personally, although I generally
try to act on the basis of enlightened self-interest, and there is little
profit in the revenge business (personal that is, there is lots of profit
in doing it for hire), I am not above trashing somebody who really annoys
me with a physical, financial or reputational attack.
